        "math_academy_id": "q-126370",
        "problem": "Find the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=1,$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2!}{(x-1)}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{3!}{(x-1)}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(1)}{4!}{(x-1)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2034}(1)$ and ${f}^{(4)}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} & \u27f9 &  & f(1) & =\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{-5/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2034}(1) & =\\frac{3}{8}(1)^{-5/2}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{(4)}(1) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}=-\\frac{15}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3!}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{15}{16}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4!}(x-1)^{4} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{5}{128}(x-1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
